.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-VM-SNAPSHOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-vm-snapshot
.Nd snapshot every VM dataset, and prune the old ones
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -root Ar dataset
.Op Fl -keep Ar count
.Op Fl -prefix Ar string
.Op Fl n
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
takes one ZFS snapshot of every dataset and zvol beneath a VM root, then prunes
that prefix back to a retention count.
It is what
.Pa kvm-snapshot.service
runs, hourly, on any machine that hosts virtual machines.
.Pp
Every snapshot in a run shares a single UTC timestamp, so an operator restoring
a set of machines can name one moment and get all of them from it rather than
reconciling eight datasets taken seconds apart.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -root Ar dataset
The dataset whose children are snapshotted.
The default is
.Pa rpool/vms .
The root itself is never snapshotted: it is a container, and an hourly snapshot
of it is an entry no restore has ever used.
.Pp
.Dl # kldload-vm-snapshot --root rpool/snaptest
.Dl kldload-vm-snapshot: 3 of 3 datasets snapshotted @auto-20260917T051854Z
.It Fl -keep Ar count
How many snapshots carrying this prefix to retain per dataset, oldest pruned
first.
The default is 48, which is two days at the hourly cadence.
.Pp
.Dl # kldload-vm-snapshot --root rpool/snaptest --keep 2
.Dl kldload-vm-snapshot: 3 of 3 datasets snapshotted @auto-20260917T051856Z, 3 pruned
.It Fl -prefix Ar string
The snapshot name prefix.
The default is
.Ar auto- .
Pruning only ever considers snapshots carrying the prefix it was given, so a
run with a different prefix cannot remove another's, and named snapshots such
as
.Ar @golden
are never at risk.
.Pp
.Dl # kldload-vm-snapshot --prefix nightly- --keep 7
.Dl \&... # a second, independently pruned series alongside the hourly one
.It Fl n , Fl -dry-run
Print what would be snapshotted and change nothing.
Pruning is skipped entirely in this mode.
.Pp
.Dl $ sudo kldload-vm-snapshot -n
.Dl "  would snapshot rpool/vms/kldload-w-2@auto-20260917T051853Z"
.Dl "  would snapshot rpool/vms/kldload-w-3@auto-20260917T051853Z"
.Dl kldload-vm-snapshot: 23 of 23 datasets snapshotted @auto-20260917T051853Z
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/lib/systemd/system/kvm-snapshot.service
The unit that runs this hourly.
.It Pa /usr/lib/systemd/system/kvm-snapshot.timer
The hourly trigger.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Every dataset was snapshotted.
A machine with no VM root, or a VM root with no children, also exits 0 after
saying so: neither is a failure, and both are normal on a machine that has not
hosted a virtual machine yet.
.It 1
One or more datasets could not be snapshotted.
Each one is named on standard error.
.It 2
A usage error, such as a
.Fl -keep
that is not a number.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It "N of M datasets snapshotted"
The count is deliberately reported against the number of datasets the run was
.Em given ,
not merely the number it succeeded at.
.Sy 0 of 23
has to read as a failure at a glance; for a long time it did not.
.It "no rpool/vms on this machine \(em nothing to snapshot"
There is no VM root here.
Reported rather than passed over in silence, because a machine that was
supposed to be a VM host and quietly is not looks exactly the same as one that
never was.
.It "FAILED to snapshot Ar dataset"
That dataset could not be snapshotted; the run continues with the rest and
exits non-zero at the end.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kfire 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-delete 8 ,
.Xr zfs 8
.Sh HISTORY
This logic used to live inside
.Pa kvm-snapshot.service
as a one-line
.Va ExecStart= .
systemd expands
.Va ${...}
in an Exec line before the program runs, so the variables holding the timestamp
and the dataset name were emptied on the way through and the command that
actually ran, every hour, was
.Ql zfs snapshot @auto- .
.Xr zfs 8
printed its usage message, the last command in the chain succeeded anyway, and
the unit reported
.Ql Finished ... Deactivated successfully
on every run since the machine was installed.
.Pp
It was found on 2026-09-16 on a host with twenty-three VM datasets and exactly
zero snapshots, whose timer had last fired seventeen minutes earlier.
Moving the logic into a program was the fix: the escaping is one
.Ql $$
away from returning, and an Exec line cannot be tested on its own.
.Sh CAVEATS
A snapshot is not a backup.
These are local, on the same pool as the machines they protect, and they
survive nothing that takes the pool with it.
Replication is what makes them a backup; see
.Xr syncoid 1 .
